`-` is the one non-chunk a call's producer position admits: a service origin's
`@rpc` has no producer chunk (RFC 06 §5), so `service call @catalog - …` names
none. Against a host or the fleet it is this tool's own refusal — still a 2,
and still before a session.

```console
$ zenctl service call h-0123456789ab - introspect
? 2
Error: producer `-` stands for no producer chunk, which only a service origin (`@…`) has (RFC 06 §5) — a host or the fleet needs the producer's name

```

```console
$ zenctl bench rpc '*' -
? 2
each call times out after 5.0s — a p99 at or near that number is measuring the timeout, not the fleet
Error: producer `-` stands for no producer chunk, which only a service origin (`@…`) has (RFC 06 §5) — a host or the fleet needs the producer's name

```

`node info` takes an origin id and refuses a hostname (RFC 06 §6) — a refusal
of the input, so the same 2. It used to be a 1, the code reserved for a
finding.

```console
$ zenctl node info MyHost
? 2
Error: "MyHost" is not an origin id — a hostname must be resolved to its origin first (RFC 06 §6); `zenctl node list` shows the roster

```
